S^{\\rm(EOS)}_{\\rm EM}(z,Z)=\\frac{N^{\\rm(EM)}_{\\rm merger}(z,Z)}{N_{\\rm merger} (z,Z)}  is the fraction of merging systems that have an EM counterpart (\\tilde{M}_{\\rm em}>0), assuming a given EOS. To compute BH-NS merger rates (considering both systems with and without EM counterparts), \\Gamma_{\\rm BH-NS}(z), we simply impose S^{\\rm(EOS)}_{\\rm EM}(z,Z)=1.

## 3. Results

For a given EOS, whether a NS plunges directly onto a BH or is tidally disrupted producing an EM counterpart depends crucially on the BH spin and its orientation with respect to the orbital angular momentum. In Figure 2, the region below each line covers the portion of the mass (M_{\\rm NS}-M_{\\rm BH}) parameter space that allows the tidal disruption of a NS by a BH for each combination of NS EOSs and BH spin models considered in this work. We also report the 90% credible interval for the LVK candidate event GW190426, the two confirmed LVK BH-NS mergers GW201005 and GW20015, and the e-analysis by Mandel and Smith (2021) of GW200115 (GW200115 M21). Note that, for the BH masses of interest here, \\chi_{\\rm BH}=0.9 and \\chi_{\\rm BH}=0.1 correspond to the Geneva model and the mesa model, respectively, while \\chi_{\\rm BH}=0.0 to the model of Fuller and Ma (2019) (see Figure 3 in Baner

